        passageEn:
            "In this invented legend Kaung Htet wrote for his Term 6 project, a boy named Kaung Htet set out from his village to find the ancient bell that had once rung to protect Nova Village from floods, but had vanished into the forest fifty years ago.\n\nBefore leaving, Kaung Htet visited the old hermit who lived at the edge of the forest, a mentor who warned him that the path was guarded by a river spirit who tested the courage of every traveller. Take this carved whistle, the hermit said, and blow it three times if the spirit tries to stop you.\n\nDeep in the forest, the river spirit rose from the water and blocked Kaung Htet's path, demanding to know why a mere boy dared to seek the lost bell. Kaung Htet blew the whistle exactly as the hermit had instructed, and the spirit, recognising the hermit's mark, allowed him to pass.\n\nAt last, Kaung Htet found the bell hanging from a fallen tree, and he carried it back to Nova Village, where it was hung once more in the temple tower. From that day on, the villagers say the bell rings on its own whenever floodwaters threaten, thanks to the boy brave enough to complete the quest his grandfather's grandfather had failed.",
        passageMy:
