            "On the final evening of Term 8, Nova School's hall filled with parents and students for the drama club's performance of The Old Library Tower, the play Kaung Htet had spent the whole term adapting, revising, and rehearsing.\n\nStanding backstage, Kaung Htet felt his heart pounding the way his character's heart pounded on the staircase in Scene One, and he suddenly understood the story from the inside in a way he never had while just writing it. When his cue came, he stepped on stage, remembered to project his voice to the back row, and let his character's eager, curious energy carry him through the opening lines exactly as he and Ms. Thanda had rehearsed.\n\nHalfway through the performance, Su Su's careful, trembling delivery of her nervous lines drew a hush from the audience, proof that the character voices they had worked so hard to separate were doing exactly what they were meant to do. When the final line was spoken and the lights dimmed, the hall erupted into applause.\n\nAfterwards, Ms. Thanda told Kaung Htet that watching a story move from a page he had written, through stage directions and rehearsed dialogue, to a performance an entire audience could feel, was the whole reason playwriting existed -- and that this term, he had learned to do all of it himself.",
