        passageEn:
            "For his Term 9 project, Kaung Htet chose to write a short biography of his grandmother, the same grandmother who had first told him the myth of the Ayeyarwady River back in Term 6.\n\nDaw Khin Myo was born in Nova Village in 1958 and spent her childhood listening to her own grandmother's stories by lamplight, long before electricity reached the village. At nineteen, she became the village's youngest schoolteacher, and she began collecting local myths and legends from elders so they would not be forgotten as Nova Village modernised.\n\nHer greatest achievement, according to Kaung Htet's research, came in 1995, when she published a small handwritten collection of Nova Village folk tales that the village school still uses today. Kaung Htet included this fact carefully, since a biography should highlight a person's most significant accomplishments rather than every detail of their life.\n\nKaung Htet ended his biography by noting that his grandmother's storytelling had shaped his own love of writing, connecting her life story directly to his own.",
