            "For the Term 3 capstone project, Thiha and his group had to plan the loops needed for the Nova School End-of-Term Fair using both loop types they had learned.\n\nFor handing out exactly thirty-six raffle tickets, they wrote a count-controlled loop: repeat 36 times: give one ticket to the next visitor. For announcing prizes, since they did not know in advance how many rounds it would take before every prize was collected, they wrote a condition-controlled loop instead: repeat until all prizes are collected: announce the next prize number.\n\nDuring rehearsal, their prize-announcing loop refused to stop -- the group had forgotten to actually mark a prize as \"collected\" once it was handed out, so the condition could never become true. Thiha and his group debugged the mistake together, adding the missing step, and the loop finally stopped correctly once the last prize was handed out.\n\nMs. Thanda told the class that this single bug -- a condition that could never become true -- was the exact mistake that caused most infinite loops, and that catching it through careful testing was exactly what computational thinking looked like.",
