            "During computing class, Ms. Thanda asked the students to trace exactly what happens when Thiha sends a chat message to his classmate Aye Aye across the Nova School network.\n\nThiha typed his message and pressed send. Instantly, his device broke the message down into several small data packets, each one carrying a piece of the message along with important address information about where it needed to go.\n\nThe packets left Thiha's laptop and reached the school's router, which read the address information on each packet and directed it toward Aye Aye's device on the same network. Because the router knew exactly where to send each packet, the message did not get lost or sent to the wrong classroom.\n\nWithin a fraction of a second, all the packets arrived at Aye Aye's device and were reassembled in the correct order, turning back into Thiha's original readable message. Ms. Thanda explained that this same process -- breaking data into packets, routing them, and reassembling them -- happens billions of times every second across the entire internet, connecting devices all over the world.",
