            "For the Term 8 capstone, Thiha's class combined everything they had learned about hardware, the IPO cycle, and sensors to expand the greenhouse project into a full Nova School Smart Garden.\n\nInside a small control box, the team installed a processing board that worked like a miniature CPU, along with a bit of memory to temporarily hold sensor readings while they were being checked, similar to how RAM works inside a full-sized computer. Every wire connected back to this board, just like every hardware part of a desktop computer connects through its motherboard.\n\nAround the garden, the team placed their trusted trio of sensors -- temperature, light, and soil moisture -- constantly sending input into the control box. The board processed each reading and decided, moment by moment, whether any output action was needed: opening a vent, turning on a grow light, or starting the water pump.\n\nOn the day of the showcase, Thiha explained to visiting parents that the Smart Garden was not really one invention, but the same input-process-output pattern from the smart doorbell, powered by the same kind of hardware from the old computer tower, and guided by the same monitoring-versus-control thinking from the greenhouse sensors -- all working together, all summarized in one living, growing project.",
        passageMy:
