        passageEn:
            "Zin Min, a Year 5 student at Nova School, spent his Saturday morning helping his uncle count sacks of rice at Bayintnaung wholesale market in Yangon. His uncle owned a small rice trading stall, and every week he needed to record exactly how many sacks arrived from different regions and how much each shipment was worth in kyat. \"Numbers here are never small,\" his uncle laughed, handing Zin Min a thick ledger book. \"You will need everything you know about place value today.\"\n\nThe first shipment from the Ayeyarwady Delta was recorded as 154,320 kyat worth of rice. Zin Min looked carefully at each digit: the 1 stood for one hundred thousand, the 5 stood for fifty thousand, the 4 stood for four thousand, the 3 stood for three hundred, the 2 stood for twenty, and the 0 stood for zero ones. He proudly told his uncle that the whole number could be broken into expanded form as 100,000 + 50,000 + 4,000 + 300 + 20 + 0. A second shipment from Mandalay was worth 148,750 kyat, and a third from Bago was worth exactly 500,001 kyat -- just barely over half a million, Zin Min noted, comparing it against the other totals.\n\nBy midday, Zin Min's uncle asked him to add up all three shipments and compare the total against last month's grand total of 999,999 kyat. Zin Min carefully lined up the digits by place value, added them column by column, and discovered the new total crossed one million kyat for the very first time. \"Once you pass 999,999 and add just one more,\" Zin Min explained to his younger cousin, \"you get a brand new place value -- the millions place -- and the number becomes 1,000,000.\" His uncle beamed with pride, telling Zin Min that understanding place value in numbers this large was exactly the skill a good trader needed.",
