            "At the end of Term 1, Nova School held its first Number Masters Challenge, a four-station puzzle event in the school hall where every Year 5 student had to solve one problem at each station before racing to the finish line. Zin Min, buzzing with excitement, lined up at Station 1 with his classmates.\n\nStation 1 tested place value: the puzzle card showed 468,213 kyat raised so far for the school's new library shelves, and Zin Min had to identify that the digit 6 stood for 60,000, using the same place-value skill he had practised counting rice sacks earlier in the term. At Station 2, a thermometer card showed the challenge tent had started at 6°C that morning and dropped 9°C overnight, and Zin Min quickly used his negative-number skill to calculate 6 − 9 = -3°C before moving on. Station 3 asked him to estimate the total number of visitors expected across two upcoming school events, 3,850 and 2,190, by rounding each to the nearest 1,000 first -- giving 4,000 + 2,000 = 6,000 -- exactly the rounding skill he had used for the Charity Walk report.\n\nThe final station, Station 4, was brand new: a locked puzzle box marked with the Roman numeral CDXIV, and Zin Min had to work out its value to unlock it. He broke it apart carefully -- CD equalled 400, X equalled 10, and IV equalled 4 -- and added them together to get 414. The box clicked open to reveal the winning team's certificate. Zin Min's team finished first, and his teacher told the whole hall that they had just used four terms' worth of number skills -- place value, negative numbers, rounding, and now Roman numerals -- all in a single afternoon, proving that every new topic in mathematics builds on the ones that came before it.",
