        passageEn:
            "At the end of every market week, Zin Min's uncle sat down with his ledger book and worked out whether the rice stall had made a profit or a loss on each day, and Zin Min had started helping him total the figures using negative numbers instead of simply writing \"profit\" or \"loss\" in words. \"A loss is just a negative number,\" his uncle explained. \"If we spend more than we earn, we write it with a minus sign, and it helps us see exactly how far below zero we have fallen.\"\n\nOn Monday, heavy rain kept customers away, and the stall recorded a loss of 2,300 kyat, so Zin Min wrote -2,300 in the ledger. On Tuesday, a bag of rice spoiled in storage and had to be thrown away, adding another loss of 1,050 kyat, bringing the running total to -2,300 + (-1,050) = -3,350 kyat. Zin Min's uncle was worried, but by Wednesday, a large regular customer returned and bought several sacks at once, earning the stall a profit of 4,500 kyat. Zin Min added this gain to the running total: -3,350 + 4,500 = 1,150 kyat, and the stall was finally back above zero.\n\nBy the end of the week, an unusually large export order pushed the stall's profit up to 12,000 kyat for Saturday alone, and the week's grand total climbed to 13,150 kyat. Zin Min's uncle asked him to work out the difference between the worst day of the week (-3,350 kyat on Tuesday evening) and the best single day (12,000 kyat on Saturday). Zin Min drew a number line, counted 3,350 steps up to zero and then 12,000 more steps up to 12,000, giving an interval of 15,350 kyat between the stall's lowest and highest points that week. \"You have understood exactly why we need negative numbers in business,\" his uncle said proudly. \"Without them, we would never know how deep a loss really goes, or how far we climbed back out of it.\"",
