            "As class monitor, Zin Min was given an important job: work out exactly how much it would cost to order new exercise books for the whole of Year 6 at Nova School. The school office told him each box contained enough exercise books for one class and cost 1,350 kyat, and the school needed 24 boxes in total.\n\nZin Min set up the long multiplication carefully, multiplying 1,350 by the ones digit of 24 first, then by the tens digit, and adding the two partial products together. 1,350 x 4 gave 5,400, and 1,350 x 20 gave 27,000; adding the partial products together, 5,400 + 27,000, gave a total of 32,400 kyat. Before writing the number down in the order form, Zin Min checked his work by rounding: 1,350 rounds to about 1,400, and 24 rounds to about 25, so 1,400 x 25 gives roughly 35,000 kyat -- reasonably close to his exact answer of 32,400 kyat, which told him his long multiplication was very likely correct.\n\nThe school office then asked for a second order: 18 boxes of coloured pencils at 875 kyat per box. Zin Min multiplied 875 by 8 to get 7,000, and 875 by 10 to get 8,750, adding the partial products for a total of 15,750 kyat. Adding both orders together, 32,400 + 15,750, Zin Min worked out that Nova School would need to spend 48,150 kyat altogether on new school supplies. His teacher praised him for using long multiplication so carefully and for remembering to check every big calculation with a quick estimate first.",
