        passageEn:
            "With end-of-term exams approaching, Nova School organised an Arithmetic Mastery Studio, a special after-school workshop where Year 6 students practised solving multi-step problems exactly like those expected in the real exam. Zin Min arrived eager to test himself against the trickiest number puzzles the teacher could prepare.\n\nThe first challenge combined several operations in a single expression: 250 ÷ 5 + 3 x 7. Zin Min remembered BODMAS immediately -- division and multiplication before addition -- so he worked out 250 ÷ 5 = 50 and 3 x 7 = 21 separately, then added them together to get 71. He double-checked by estimating: 250 ÷ 5 is close to 50, and 3 x 7 is exactly 21, so 50 + 21 = 71 seemed entirely reasonable.\n\nThe second challenge was a number theory puzzle about the sports day: 48 students from one class and 60 students from another needed to be split into teams of exactly the same size, with every student included and no one left over. Zin Min listed the factors of both numbers and found the highest common factor, or HCF, was 12 -- meaning the largest possible equal team size was 12 students per team, giving four teams from the class of 48 and five teams from the class of 60.\n\nThe final puzzle asked when two school bells, one ringing every 4 minutes and one every 6 minutes, would next ring together, assuming they had both just rung at the same moment. Zin Min listed the multiples of 4 and 6 side by side until he found the lowest common multiple, or LCM, which was 12 -- so the bells would ring together again in exactly 12 minutes. Walking home afterwards, Zin Min realised that factors, multiples, HCF, and LCM were not just abstract number theory -- they were the exact tools needed to plan a fair sports day and to predict something as ordinary as two bells ringing together.",
