        passageEn:
            "For the Year 6 science fair, Zin Min planted a mung bean seed in a small cup on his windowsill and measured its height with a ruler every single morning for eight days, recording each measurement carefully in his science journal. On average, his plant grew by a steady 0.6 cm every day.\n\nTo predict the total height after the full eight days without measuring again, Zin Min multiplied the daily growth by the number of days: 0.6 x 8 = 4.8 cm. He checked this against his actual final measurement of 4.8 cm and was delighted that his multiplication matched the real plant perfectly.\n\nHis classmate, Su Su, had grown a different bean plant that reached a total height of 9.6 cm over the same eight days. Curious about whose plant grew faster on average, Zin Min divided Su Su's total growth by the number of days: 9.6 ÷ 8 = 1.2 cm per day, which was exactly double his own daily average of 0.6 cm.\n\nWhen their teacher compared the two journals, she wrote a note in fraction form: \"Su Su's plant grew three-quarters of a centimetre more per day than Zin Min's.\" Zin Min converted the fraction to a decimal to check the note: 3/4 = 0.75 cm, and indeed 0.6 + 0.75 = 1.35 cm, which was close to -- though not exactly -- Su Su's measured 1.2 cm per day, since real plants never grow in perfectly steady amounts every single day. \"Decimals and fractions are just two different ways of writing the very same growing bean sprout,\" Zin Min told Su Su, comparing their two journals side by side.",
        passageMy:
