            "For his final Year 6 project, Zin Min designed a complete statistical investigation from start to finish, choosing a question that genuinely interested him: 'How long do students in my school spend on homework each evening?'\n\nFirst, Zin Min carefully worded his survey question to avoid bias, settling on 'How many minutes did you spend on homework yesterday evening?' rather than a leading question like 'Don't you think homework takes too long?' He then chose a fair sample: 30 students, picked randomly from every class in Year 6, rather than just his own close friends.\n\nOnce the data was collected, Zin Min had to decide how to represent it. For a simple breakdown of students by homework-time category (under 30 minutes, 30-60 minutes, over 60 minutes), a pie chart worked perfectly: 12 students fell under 30 minutes, 12 fell between 30-60 minutes, and 6 fell over 60 minutes, giving angles of 144°, 144°, and 72°.\n\nTo find the overall average time spent, Zin Min calculated the mean of all 30 recorded times, adding every value together and dividing by 30, arriving at a mean homework time of 42 minutes across the whole sample.\n\nFinally, Zin Min wrote his conclusion: most students spent between 30 and 60 minutes on homework, with a school-wide average of 42 minutes, and no single class seemed dramatically different from the rest. \"A good statistical investigation is a full journey,\" Zin Min told his teacher, \"from a fair question, to a fair sample, to the right chart, to an honest conclusion -- exactly the kind of thinking I'll need for secondary school and beyond.\"",
