        passageEn:
            "With the big end-of-year assessment only weeks away, Zin Min pulled out every maths notebook he had filled since the very first week of Year 6 and decided to test himself with one enormous combined problem, gathering ideas from the whole year in a single calculation.\n\nThe problem began with a number from a school fundraising total: 4,536,208 kyat. Zin Min identified the value of the digit 5 straight away -- 500,000 -- remembering the very same place-value skill from his first-ever Term 1 lesson about numbers up to ten million.\n\nNext, the problem asked him to imagine the fund being multiplied by a small grant-matching scheme: a simpler version used 216 x 34 as practice, and Zin Min carefully worked through the long multiplication, getting 216 x 34 = 7,344, laying out his partial products just as he had learned back in Term 2.\n\nThen came a division check: 7,344 ÷ 34 needed to bring him back to 216 exactly, confirming his multiplication was correct -- and sure enough, long division gave 216 with no remainder.\n\nThe final step combined everything using BODMAS: 500,000 + 216 x 34 - 7,344 ÷ 34. Working through the order of operations carefully, Zin Min calculated the multiplication and division first, 216 x 34 = 7,344 and 7,344 ÷ 34 = 216, then finished with the addition and subtraction: 500,000 + 7,344 - 216 = 507,128.\n\nLooking back at how confidently he had solved every single stage, Zin Min realised just how far he had come since the very first week of Term 1, when place value alone had felt like the hardest thing in the world.",
        passageMy:
