        passageEn:
            "Hnin Wai is a Year 5 student at Nova School, and every Saturday morning she helps her grandmother cook breakfast. One morning, Hnin Wai notices something strange: the metal spoon left standing in the hot pot becomes too hot to touch within minutes, while the wooden spoon resting beside it stays cool enough to pick up straight away. She decides to turn this kitchen puzzle into a real science investigation for her school science club.\n\nBack at Nova School, Hnin Wai sets up a fair test with her teacher's help. She places a metal spoon, a wooden spoon, and a plastic spoon into the same cup of hot water at the same time, making sure each spoon is the same size and the water is the same temperature to keep the test fair. After two minutes, she touches the top of each spoon handle carefully. The metal spoon is noticeably warmer than the wooden and plastic spoons. Hnin Wai records her results in a table and explains to her classmates that metal is a good thermal conductor, which is why it heats up quickly, while wood and plastic are thermal insulators that stay cooler for longer.\n\nLater that day, the science club builds simple circuits using a battery, a bulb, and short wires with crocodile clips. Hnin Wai tests different objects by connecting them into the circuit: a metal paperclip makes the bulb light up brightly, but a rubber band and a strip of plastic keep the bulb dark. \"The paperclip is a good electrical conductor,\" Hnin Wai tells the group, \"but the rubber band is an electrical insulator, so no current can flow through it.\" By the end of the afternoon, Hnin Wai has filled two pages of her notebook comparing thermal and electrical conductivity, proud that her simple kitchen observation grew into a proper science experiment.",
