            "For a class project at Nova School, Hnin Wai and her group were asked to design two paper boats and race them across a long plastic tub of water by blowing on them with a straw. The first boat Hnin Wai folded was wide, flat, and boxy. The second boat had a narrow, pointed front and a smooth, tapered body, just like the shapes she had seen in pictures of dolphins and racing cars.\n\nWhen the race began, the boxy boat wobbled and moved slowly, pushed back by the water piling up against its flat front. The streamlined boat, however, sliced through the water smoothly and reached the other end of the tub first. \"That's because your boat is streamlined,\" the teacher explained, \"its narrow, pointed shape lets water flow past it with much less resistance, just like a dolphin's body moving through the sea.\"\n\nHnin Wai's group then tried adding a tiny drop of cooking oil to the underside of the boxy boat, as an experiment in lubrication -- reducing friction between the boat and the tub's plastic surface where it dragged along the bottom. The boat slid a little more easily, though it still could not beat the streamlined design. Hnin Wai concluded that shape mattered more than lubrication alone for cutting through water, and she couldn't wait to test whether the same streamlined shape would also win a race through moving air.",
