        passageEn:
            "During morning break at Nova School, Hnin Wai noticed that her Year 5 science class had been asked to find three real levers on the playground before recess ended. She grabbed her notebook, drew a small table with the headings Load, Effort, and Fulcrum, and set off to look around the equipment near the sandpit.\n\nThe first lever she found was easy: the seesaw. Hnin Wai sat on one end while her friend Thiri sat on the other, and the metal bar in the middle stayed perfectly still while both ends went up and down. She wrote down that the bar in the middle was the fulcrum, sitting exactly between the two children, who took turns being the load and providing the effort. That made it a first-class lever, just like the diagram her teacher had shown the class the day before.\n\nNext, Hnin Wai spotted the school gardener using a wheelbarrow to move a pile of cut branches to the compost corner. She watched carefully as he lifted the two handles at the back; the wheel at the front stayed on the ground and did not move up or down. Hnin Wai realised the wheel was the fulcrum, the branches piled in the middle were the load, and the gardener's hands lifting the handles were the effort — with the load sitting between the fulcrum and the effort, this was a second-class lever, and it let the gardener move a much heavier pile of branches than he could ever carry by hand. Pleased with her two examples, Hnin Wai ran to find a pair of scissors in the art room for her third lever before the bell rang.",
