            "For the science fair, Hnin Wai wanted to build a simple torch using a battery, a bulb, a switch, and some wire, but her first attempt would not light up at all.\n\nMs. Su Su asked her to trace the path of the circuit with her finger, starting at the battery. Hnin Wai followed the wire from the battery to the switch, but noticed the wire on the other side of the switch did not actually touch the bulb's metal casing -- there was a small gap. Because the loop was not complete, the circuit could not work, no matter how good the battery or bulb were.\n\nAfter reconnecting the wire so it made a solid connection to the bulb, Hnin Wai flipped the switch, and the bulb lit up instantly. She tested it by opening the switch again, and the light went out immediately, proving that the switch really was controlling whether the loop was open or closed.\n\nHnin Wai realized that every single component in a circuit had one job, but all of them had to be connected in an unbroken loop for any of those jobs to matter. A perfect battery and a perfect bulb were useless if even one connection in between was broken.",
