        passageEn:
            "Nova School was holding a special morning assembly, and Aye Aye had been chosen to present a short talk on Greek mythology and the Olympic Games as part of her class's humanities unit. She practised in front of her mirror the night before, holding a rolled-up piece of paper like a torch, imagining she was carrying it into an ancient stadium.\n\nAye Aye began her talk by explaining that the ancient Greeks believed twelve powerful gods lived together on Mount Olympus, the tallest mountain in Greece. Zeus was their king, ruling the sky and throwing lightning bolts when he was angry, while his daughter Athena was the goddess of wisdom and clever strategy. Aye Aye told her classmates that these gods appeared throughout Greek myths and epics, including Homer's Odyssey, a long poem about the hero Odysseus and his ten-year journey home after the Trojan War, filled with monsters, storms, and clever tricks.\n\nThen Aye Aye connected the myths to something her audience already loved: sport. She explained that the Ancient Olympic Games began as a religious festival held every four years at Olympia to honour Zeus, and that during the games, Greek city-states — even rivals like Athens and Sparta — agreed to a special truce, pausing any wars so that athletes and spectators could travel safely to compete and watch. Winners were crowned not with gold medals but with a simple wreath of olive leaves, yet they were celebrated as heroes back in their home cities for the rest of their lives. As Aye Aye finished her talk, she told her classmates that the Olympic torch relay used in the modern games today is a tradition inspired directly by the flame the ancient Greeks kept burning in honour of Zeus, connecting sport across thousands of years.",
